<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Baden</h1><p class="st-meta">1112 CE – 1803 CE · capital Baden-Baden; later Durlach, Karlsruhe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south-west German margraviate on the upper Rhine, divided for two centuries, reunited in 1771 and later the Grand Duchy of Baden.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-ii-margrave-of-baden">Hermann II</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringen</a> family, first called himself margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1112, after the castle of Baden near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-baden">Baden-Baden</a>. The title derived from his family's claim to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/march-of-verona">March of Verona</a>. The margraves built lands in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> plain and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1535 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> split into Catholic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-baden">Baden-Baden</a> and Protestant <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Baden-Durlach</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-william-margrave-of-baden-baden">Louis William of Baden-Baden</a>, the 'Turkish Louis', fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt-palace">Rastatt Palace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-william-margrave-of-baden-durlach">Charles III William</a> of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> in 1715, a planned city radiating from the palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-frederick-grand-duke-of-baden">Charles Frederick</a>, an enlightened ruler, abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> in 1783.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The two lines reunited under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-frederick-grand-duke-of-baden">Charles Frederick</a> in 1771. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> reorganisation tripled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden's</a> size: it became an electorate in 1803 and a grand duchy in 1806. Today it forms part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-baden.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Baden" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Baden_(1%5E1).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1112 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Baden-Baden; later Durlach, Karlsruhe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermann II of Baden, Louis William of Baden-Baden, Charles Frederick of Baden</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic and Franconian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Markgrafschaft Baden</dd></dl></aside></div>
